! ============================================================
! 04d  Following an instability down
! ============================================================
! The same stretched H2 as 04c, but with %stability follow
! instead of check.
!
! The difference matters.  "check" diagnoses and stops;
! "follow" rotates the orbitals along the most negative
! eigenvector, rebuilds the density and re-converges the SCF,
! repeating until that channel is positive definite.
!
! WHAT TO LOOK AT: only the INTERNAL (real singlet) channel is
! followed.  On this system that channel was already stable,
! so nothing happens and the run simply confirms it -- which
! is the honest outcome, and worth seeing.  The external
! triplet instability is reported again and left alone, for
! the reason given in 04c.
!
! TO SEE "follow" DO SOMETHING, you need a case with an
! internal instability: a symmetry-broken solution of a
! conjugated ring, or a transition-metal complex trapped in an
! excited configuration.  Those are beyond a five-second
! example, but the mechanics are the same as here.
!
! %stability_maxcycle caps the rotate-and-reconverge loop.
! %stability_step is the rotation amplitude; the default 0.35
! rad is a compromise between escaping a narrow attractor and
! landing somewhere unrelated.  If the energy RISES after a
! rotation the run says so -- the usual cause is a fixed
! %level_shift pulling the SCF straight back into the basin
! it came from.
%method  HF
%basis   6-31G
%job     energy
%charge  0
%mult    1
%stability          follow
%stability_maxcycle 10
%stability_step     0.35

%geo
 H   0.0  0.0  0.0
 H   0.0  0.0  2.5
